# THIS_FILE_IS_MANAGED_BY_ANSIBLE__DO_NOT_EDIT_MANUALLY
# X11 display-server access (high risk) - /etc/apparmor.d/abstractions/local_mixin_gui_x11
#
# TL;DR - include in: any GUI app that may run on X11 / XWayland, ONLY inside the
#         `{% if apparmor_allow_x11 %}` branch. Pair it with local_mixin_gui_x11_deny in the
#         matching `{% else %}`. (Exception: an app that hard-requires X11 regardless of the
#         toggle - e.g. a remote-control tool - includes this UNCONDITIONALLY, with a comment.)
#
# Grants:
#   - owner /run/user/*/xauth_* r           X server auth cookie (the key to the display)
#   - /tmp/.X11-unix/ r                      list the X socket directory
#   - /tmp/.X11-unix/X* rw                   connect to the X server's filesystem socket
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# X11 has NO inter-client isolation: a client that reaches the X server can read every
# window, globally log keyboard / mouse input, read the clipboard, screenshot the whole
# desktop, and inject events via XTEST. The xauth cookie is the key to all of that, so this
# entire mixin is gated behind apparmor_allow_x11 (default false). Contrast
# local_mixin_gui_wayland (compositor-isolated, low risk, ungated).
#
# Two socket styles, ONE mixin on purpose:
#   - /tmp/.X11-unix/X*   filesystem socket (legacy Qt / GTK XWayland clients).
#   - the abstract socket @/tmp/.X11-unix/X0 is mediated by `network unix` (granted by
#                         local_mixin_network_local_ipc), so it needs no file rule here.
# Both reach the SAME X server. Apps that use only the abstract socket still get the
# filesystem path here - a redundant second door to a room they already hold the key (xauth)
# to, so it widens nothing.

owner /run/user/*/xauth_* r,
/tmp/.X11-unix/ r,
/tmp/.X11-unix/X* rw,
